# clang-tidy, held to zero. The checks were picked from a tree-wide probe -- the compilation
# units the database names, the same run scripts/quality/tidy.sh gates on -- rather than from
# taste: what fires today is fixed or declared deliberate, and what does not fire is kept, so
# a future violation is new code and the gate names the change that brought it. The list only
# grows, like every other ratchet in this repository.
#
# Two entries answer for whole classes rather than single sites, and each says why:
#
#   bugprone-unchecked-optional-access -- the tree reads optionals through value() on purpose:
#     the empty path is a loud exception instead of undefined behaviour, and the happy path is
#     byte-for-byte what it was. The check treats value() exactly like operator->, so every
#     one of those reads is a finding however it is spelled. The IgnoreValueCalls option that
#     would say "value() is the checked accessor here" arrives in LLVM 21; on 19 and 20 an
#     unknown CheckOption is swallowed silently, which would make the config look like it
#     answers a question it does not, so the check stays off in the list until the toolchain
#     moves and the option is real.
#
#   performance-enum-size -- every enum carries an explicit underlying type (: int, which is
#     what it already was) and the persisted ones' storage does not move, so the size question
#     is answered at each definition. EnumIgnoreList turns the check off at the option rather
#     than in the Checks list, which keeps the door open for naming a single enum whose size
#     should be argued instead of defaulted.
#
# clang-analyzer covers the class the crash dumps keep finding -- a null or emptied handle read
# after its owner retired it. It is the expensive half, which is why CI runs it only over the
# files a change touches rather than over the tree. Two of its behaviours this config accounts
# for: analyzer diagnostics ignore HeaderFilterRegex (they report wherever the bug report points,
# Qt headers included), and the analyzer cannot see QMetaObject::invokeMethod taking ownership of
# a queued lambda, which is why NewDeleteLeaks reads every one of those call sites as a leak.
#
# HeaderFilterRegex names our files by their src/ or test/ segment; the same regex leaves the
# vendored headers out, and a suppressed diagnostic is not fatalised by WarningsAsErrors -- the
# gate only ever judges files in this repository. The probe found no diagnostic inside the
# googletest headers, whose paths also carry a src/ segment; if one ever appears, that is what
# this comment explains.

Checks: >
  -*,
  clang-analyzer-core.*,
  clang-analyzer-cplusplus.*,
  bugprone-*,
  performance-*,
  modernize-use-override,
  -bugprone-unchecked-optional-access
CheckOptions:
  - key:   bugprone-empty-catch.AllowEmptyCatchForExceptions
    value: winrt::hresult_error
  - key:   performance-enum-size.EnumIgnoreList
    value: .*

WarningsAsErrors: '*'

HeaderFilterRegex: '[/\\](src|test)[/\\]'
